@meta
id: 15-11
title: Reverse the file
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-move
par: 15
minutes: 5
vscode: cut the last line, paste it at the top, ten times
open: rollback.sql

@brief
The migration creates the tables, then the indexes, then the foreign
keys. Somebody wrote `rollback.sql` by copying that list and changing
`CREATE` to `DROP`, which means it tries to drop `customer` while three
other tables still point at it. A rollback has to undo things in the
opposite order to the one they were done in.

Reverse the file. Ten lines, last to first.

The command after a `:g` pattern can be any Ex command, and `:m` moves
a line. `:m0` moves it to the very top, above line 1. Think about what
happens when every line in the file, in order, is moved to the top.

@keys
| `:g/^/m0` | for every line, move it to the top |
| `:m0` | move the current line above line 1 |
| `^` | as a pattern on its own: matches every line |

@hints
The pattern needs to match every line. The shortest pattern that does
that is the start-of-line anchor with nothing after it.
--
`:m0` on line 1 does nothing. `:m0` on line 2 puts it above line 1.
`:m0` on line 3 puts it above both. Keep going.
--
`:g/^/m0` and Enter. Seven characters that reverse a file of any
length.

@solution
    :g/^/m0<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes. This one is old enough to be a vim proverb.

Walk through it once so you own the mechanism rather than the incantation.
`:g` marks all ten lines. It visits line 1 and moves it to the top:
no change. It visits what was line 2 and moves it above line 1: now the
order is 2, 1. It visits line 3: 3, 2, 1. By the time it reaches the
last mark the file is back to front. Every line was moved exactly once
and the marks kept track of which lines had been visited, which is why
moving lines around underneath the loop does not confuse it.

That fact, that `:g` marks first and then acts, is the one that makes
`:m` and its cousin `:t` safe to use inside it. Chapter 24 covers the
rest of the `:m`, `:t` and `:d` family in their own right. Here they
are only interesting because of what `:g` can do with them.

For a file with a header line to keep in place, `:2,$g/^/m1` reverses
everything below it.

@start rollback.sql
DROP TABLE customer;
DROP TABLE "order";
DROP TABLE payment;
DROP TABLE refund;
DROP INDEX order_customer_id_idx;
DROP INDEX payment_order_id_idx;
DROP INDEX refund_payment_id_idx;
ALTER TABLE "order" DROP CONSTRAINT order_customer_id_fkey;
ALTER TABLE payment DROP CONSTRAINT payment_order_id_fkey;
ALTER TABLE refund DROP CONSTRAINT refund_payment_id_fkey;

@expected rollback.sql
ALTER TABLE refund DROP CONSTRAINT refund_payment_id_fkey;
ALTER TABLE payment DROP CONSTRAINT payment_order_id_fkey;
ALTER TABLE "order" DROP CONSTRAINT order_customer_id_fkey;
DROP INDEX refund_payment_id_idx;
DROP INDEX payment_order_id_idx;
DROP INDEX order_customer_id_idx;
DROP TABLE refund;
DROP TABLE payment;
DROP TABLE "order";
DROP TABLE customer;
